= How to Read This Manual


This manual serves several internal readers, each opening it for a different reason. The whole volume read end to end gives the complete picture; the reading orders below put the relevant material first.



== For a new staff member


You are learning what the program is and how its parts fit together. Read Part 1 to understand why the program exists and what problem it solves, then Part 2 in full as the operational constitution, then Part 3 to see how a limit is actually set and where the categories stand. Part 6 then locates you in the organization: the entity structure, the firewall you are expected to hold, the pipeline that turns evidence into a published standard, and the roles around you.



== For the standards team


You set and revise limits. Your center of gravity is Part 3.1 (how a limit is set under Method v2.0), Part 5.1 (why the methodology is defensible and what an auditor may request), and Part 2.11 (the Standards Ratchet, which governs how a published limit moves after publication). Part 6.3 describes the pipeline your work runs through, from Step Zero category lock to publication, and Part 6.2 states the firewall rule that keeps the arithmetic on the workbench side and out of the public literature layer.



== For certification operations


You run baselines, assign status, and manage surveillance and corrective action. Read Part 2.3 through Part 2.9 (entry, readiness, and baseline, the five statuses, certification visibility and the readiness and corrective-action tracks, mark usage, decision rules under measurement uncertainty, data governance, and corrective and preventive action), then Part 4 in full (the lot testing schedule: risk tiers, frequency by status, reflex triggers, speciation, sampling, and chain of custody). Part 2.13 gives you the appeals and reinstatement paths.



== For executive leadership and governance


You are accountable for the program's integrity and its position in the market. Read Part 1 for the strategic frame, Part 2.1 for the principles every decision is measured against, Part 5.3 for how the program compares to the recognition landscape, and Part 5.4 and Part 6 for the governance structure, the independence arrangements, and the operating model. The Governing Principles reproduced in Part 2.1 are the five-way test against which every design decision is checked.



== For counsel and advisers


You are assessing defensibility. Read Part 5.1 (the methodology defense), Part 5.2 (the program-level adversarial read, which enumerates the anticipated attacks and the program's answer to each), Part 2.13 (the two-tier appeals structure and its independence rationale), and Part 4.6 (chain of custody and the data-integrity discipline that makes results admissible). Part 6.2 explains the architectural separation that keeps the program's evidence base independent of its commercial interest.



== The public edition


A public edition of this manual is generated from the same source with Part 6 (Program Operations) and the internal-use notice removed. It is the correct document to send to a brand, a retailer, a regulator, or a journalist. It never states a different fact from this edition; it omits only the internal-operations material and is otherwise identical.



= Orientation


This section orients a reader who is new to the program. It states in plain terms what the program is, walks the full path a brand travels from first inquiry to a certified mark, and puts the numbers and terms you will meet most often on a single page. It is a way in, not a substitute for the parts that follow: every rule summarized here is specified in full in Part 2 through Part 4.



== Start here: what the program is


The Heavy Metal Tested and Certified program does one narrow thing and does it in the open. It publishes a numeric limit for each heavy metal in each kind of product, and then it certifies individual products against those published numbers under continuous laboratory testing. The limit is published before anything is certified against it, so a brand can read the exact threshold it will be held to before it applies or spends a dollar on testing.


Three choices make the program different from the testing a team member may already have in mind. First, the number comes first: the program does not test products and then decide what counts as a pass, it publishes the pass line in advance and holds everyone to it. Second, it is continuous, not a snapshot: a product is certified across multiple production lots and then kept under surveillance for as long as it carries the mark, so the mark describes an ongoing state rather than one lucky test. Third, it tests eight metals as ten analytes, where most food certifications test four, because a limit is only as protective as the metals it actually looks for.


The program is careful about what the mark claims. It certifies that a product was tested at an accredited laboratory, at or below a published number, on a stated analyte panel, in the form it is actually sold in. It does not claim the product is safe, and a brand may not use it to claim "heavy metal free." That restraint is deliberate and it is what keeps the mark defensible.


One structural point matters from the first day. The program runs on two separate properties. The certification program, its limits, and its governance live at heavymetalcertified.com and are run by the Institute of Contaminant Standards. The scientific evidence the limits draw on lives separately at the Heavy Metal Index, heavymetalindex.com, a public library of the peer-reviewed and regulatory literature. The program reads from the Index; the Index does not endorse the program or any brand. Keeping those two apart is what lets the program cite an independent evidence base rather than its own marketing, and holding that separation is part of everyone's job here.



== The certification journey


Here is the whole path a brand travels, first contact to a certified mark. Each phase is specified in full in the part named beside it.


Before any of it, a mutual confidentiality undertaking, pre-signed by the program, is in force from first contact. The brand's own counter-signature is optional and gates nothing. This is the only binding instrument in place before certification, and it is what lets a brand test and build a baseline without creating any public record.


*Phase 0. Readiness (free and private).* A brand reads the published limit for its subcategory and tests privately against it, entering its own prior laboratory results to see exactly where it stands. Readiness is free and nonpublic: no mark, no fee, and no public record that the brand looked. It exists so that testing is a diagnostic a brand uses to build a plan, not a trap that penalizes it for checking.


*Phase 1. The three-lot baseline.* A product is certified only after a baseline of three production lots, and a single passing lot certifies nothing. A brand that already runs an accredited testing program may credit up to two of the three lots with qualifying prior results, analyte by analyte. The third, completing lot is one the program runs itself: it purchases a unit of the finished product at retail, ships it under chain of custody to an accredited laboratory, and runs the panel. Buying the product independently, rather than trusting a brand-supplied sample, is what makes the completing lot a defensible anchor for the baseline, and it is the point at which program cost first enters. Part 2.3 and Part 4.3 specify this.


*Phase 2. Application and pathway.* A qualifying three-lot baseline unlocks the certification application. On application, the program sets the product's ongoing lot-testing schedule and determines which analytes are assayed on each future lot, and the readiness engine routes the product by its baseline result: a conforming product (Status A or B) proceeds toward certification, and a product not yet meeting the standard enters the confidential readiness track to correct the cause first. A conforming brand also chooses here whether its certification will be public-facing or private. Part 2.4 covers the statuses; Part 2.5 covers the pathways and that choice.


*Phase 3. Approval and agreement.* After the program's review and approval, the binding Certification Agreement that licenses the mark is drawn up and executed. Only at this point is the product certified, and only a brand that chose the public-facing path is listed publicly. The agreement is the late instrument, not the entry ticket.


*Phase 4. The mark and surveillance.* A certified product may carry the mark under the mark-usage rules and enters continuous surveillance. How often it is tested depends on its status and risk tier, and any change to ingredients, water, equipment, packaging, formulation, or process triggers an unscheduled reflex test. Part 2.6 covers the mark; Part 4 covers the schedule.


*Phase 5. When a lot falls short.* If a certified product later fails a lot, the brand opens a corrective action, fixes the root cause, and re-tests, on a clock that tightens with severity. The brand chooses how the finding is disclosed: confidentially by default, or, electively, published on its own brand page as a documented catch-and-fix. Neither route avoids the correction, and neither shields a released lot that is above its limit. A brand that conceals a known exceedance loses its certificate. Part 2.5 and Part 2.9 specify this.


*Phase 6. The ratchet (over time).* Published limits tighten as the certified market improves, on a capped, pre-announced schedule, and never loosen. A brand can see the next move coming through a published forward forecast and plan for it. Part 2.11 specifies the mechanism.


The path in one line: a confidentiality undertaking is already in force, the brand tests privately for free, builds a three-lot baseline (two credited prior lots plus one the program buys and tests), applies once the baseline qualifies, is routed to certification or to readiness, chooses public-facing or private, and, after approval, executes the agreement that licenses the mark, then stays under surveillance and improves as the limits ratchet down.


