Speciation upgrades chromium to Tier 1 treatment when a credible hexavalent pathway exists. When speciation is performed, the speciated result becomes the compliance result for that metal, superseding the total-element value. The Technical Committee may reclassify a metal between tiers on new evidence, with a defined transition period before enforcement, and may add analytes to the panel.


*Why the panel is eight, not four.* The common industry panel tests four metals: lead, cadmium, arsenic, and mercury. The program tests those four and adds nickel, tin, aluminum, and chromium because a limit is only as protective as the metals it covers, and the four additional metals reach food and consumer products through real, documented pathways that a four-metal panel is silent on. Testing eight and publishing limits for all of them is a deliberate answer to the gap a four-metal panel leaves.


*Aggregate exposure across categories.* A single product that meets a government action level does not describe the exposure a person accumulates across a diet. A day's consumption of several infant-food categories, each at its individual FDA action level, can yield a lead intake several times the FDA interim reference level for young children. This is the reason program limits sit below government action levels rather than at them, and the reason the program calibrates across categories rather than one product at a time: aggregate exposure, not single-product compliance, is the exposure that matters for the populations the program most protects.



== 2.3 Program entry, readiness, and the three-lot baseline


Entry is gated on evidence, not on signing. From first contact a mutual confidentiality undertaking, pre-signed by the program, is in force; the brand's counter-signature is optional and gates nothing. It is the only binding instrument before certification, and it is what lets a brand run readiness and build a baseline without creating any public record. The binding Certification Agreement that licenses the mark is executed much later, after the program's approval decision (Part 2.5); a brand becomes a Licensee at that point, not at entry.


*Readiness.* Before applying, a brand tests privately against the published limit for its subcategory, entering its own prior accredited laboratory results. Readiness is free and nonpublic, produces no mark and no public applicant record, and returns exact quantitative standing rather than a bare pass or fail. It is a diagnostic a brand uses to build a plan, and it is where a brand not yet meeting the standard is routed to correct the cause before any formal evaluation.


*The three-lot baseline.* A product is certified only after a baseline of three production lots; a single passing lot certifies nothing. The three results are evaluated collectively so that inherent product variability is distinguished from an anomalous lot, and each lot is documented under chain of custody. The baseline is assembled in two parts.


_Prior-lab credit, up to two of three._ A brand that already operates an accredited testing program may credit up to two of the three baseline lots with qualifying prior results. Credit is granted analyte by analyte: a prior lot that measured only some analytes credits only those, and a nondetect counts as valid coverage for its analyte when the laboratory's quantitation limit is low enough. A prior result qualifies only when it was produced by an ISO/IEC 17025-accredited laboratory; reports a quantitation limit at or below the program's reporting limit for that analyte, which the program sets at one fifth of the published limit; is recent, within a defined recency window; is supplied as structured, machine-readable data under chain of custody; and is expressed in or reliably convertible to the row's native basis. Where a species is not measured directly, its parent total may cover it for baseline purposes: total arsenic for inorganic arsenic, total mercury for methylmercury, total chromium for hexavalent chromium. The recency window is set by product cadence rather than a single flat interval, and its exact values are being finalized under governance and counsel review; this manual therefore states the requirement, recency within the governed window, without fixing the interval, which the controlled documents will set.


_The completing lot, run by the program._ The third, completing lot is a new full-panel lot the program runs itself rather than accepting from the brand. The program purchases a unit of the finished product at retail, ships it under chain of custody to an accredited laboratory, and runs the base assay. Purchasing the product independently is what makes the completing lot a defensible, brand-independent anchor for the baseline, and it is the point at which program cost first enters: the cost is the retail price of the purchased unit, the two shipping legs, and the laboratory panel, each reconciled to actual receipts with any excess refunded, plus a fixed administrative fee. The base assay measures the eight metals as totals; speciation for inorganic arsenic, methylmercury, and hexavalent chromium is run by reflex on a trigger rather than on every completing lot (Part 4.5), and the parent totals cover those species for baseline qualification.


*What a qualifying baseline unlocks.* A baseline qualifies when a completing full-panel lot exists, at least three lots are present in total, and every required analyte for the subcategory is covered. A qualifying baseline unlocks the certification application; entry to that step is not shortened by any grandfathering arrangement, which affects fees but never the baseline gate. On application the program establishes the product's ongoing lot-testing schedule (Part 4), determines from the required-analyte rule which analytes are assayed on each future lot, and the readiness engine's route determines the pathway (Part 2.5). Initial status is assigned from the worst-performing lot in the baseline: Status A when every lot is at or below 100 percent of every limit; Status B when a lot falls between 100 and 150 percent and the exceedance is confined to Tier 2 metals; Status C when a lot exceeds 150 percent or any Tier 1 metal exceeds 100 percent; and not eligible for certification when a lot exceeds 200 percent or a result violates a regulatory limit. Percent-of-limit is the result divided by the applicable limit times 100, and the maximum across a lot's analytes drives its status.



== 2.4 The five certification statuses


The program uses a staged compliance framework so that normal variability does not become a commercial catastrophe while any product bearing the mark remains supported by evidence under a defined surveillance protocol. A status attaches to a product, not to a brand, and it is visible on the certificate. Only Status A and Status B products may display the mark.


