*Status C is graduated by severity.* Low probation (MaxPoL 150 to 175 percent) requires every-lot testing with hold-and-release, with the brand's own accredited sampling permitted subject to audit. High probation (175 to 200 percent) adds mandatory independent third-party sampling directed by the Program Operator. To exit probation, the brand must produce a defined run of consecutive passing lots at Status A or B levels (a minimum of five, set by the Program Operator) and complete all corrective-action milestones with verified effectiveness. Both conditions must be met.


*Reinstatement from suspension and revocation.* Reinstatement after Status D requires a completed corrective action with verified effectiveness, a run of consecutive compliant lots set by the Program Operator, and restored data integrity: not a fresh baseline. Revocation (Status E) requires full re-application to the program at the Program Operator's discretion.



== 2.5 Certification visibility, and the readiness and corrective-action tracks


Three distinct things are easy to confuse, and the program keeps them separate. The first is a certified brand's choice to be public-facing or private. The second is the confidential readiness track for a brand not yet meeting the standard. The third is the handling of a finding on an already-certified product. Only the first is a property of certification itself; the other two are correction contexts, one before certification and one after.



=== Transitional certification (Status B)


Transitional certification exists so that a brand with a controllable Tier 2 exceedance can enter and improve under the mark rather than being locked out. It is available on Tier 2 metals only, up to 150 percent of the limit, and never for a Tier 1 metal at any margin. A Status B product carries a mandatory corrective action opened within ten business days of the determination, a full plan within thirty days, a verification-of-effectiveness plan showing measurable reduction over subsequent lots, and a default of twelve months to reach Status A. Any lot that exceeds the 150 percent cap, involves a Tier 1 exceedance, or fails to show improvement escalates the product to Status C.



=== Public-facing and private certification


Certification and public visibility are separable. A brand that meets the standard is certified either way; whether that certification is public-facing or private is the brand's own election, and it can be made per product. Publicity after certification is consent-based: the program does not list a certified product or publish its results without the brand's authorization, given through the Certification Agreement, and the public register is therefore not a census of every certified product. This is a genuine fork, and the program states the benefits of both paths honestly rather than steering brands toward whichever one markets the program.


_Public-facing certification_ puts the mark, and where the brand wishes a public brand page, in front of consumers and retailers. It is a consumer-facing trust signal and a point of shelf differentiation; a visible entry in the register answers a retailer's heavy-metal questionnaire on its own; and it strengthens the brand's own account of testing and disclosing. It fits a brand whose buyers actively reward third-party heavy-metal certification and that wants the work to be seen.


_Private certification_ keeps the full value of certification, the certificate, the standard met, the independent tested chain of custody, without publishing results a competitor or a plaintiff's expert could mine. Here confidentiality is the point rather than an obstacle: the brand keeps competitive formulation and sourcing signals out of public view, controls the timing of any disclosure it chooses to make, and avoids creating a public per-product data point before it is ready to. It fits a brand that wants the assurance and audit-readiness of certification, and the litigation-insurance value of a documented independent record, without a public standing that category competitors, retailers, or class-action researchers can read off a register.


One honesty is load-bearing and applies to both paths: privacy is a choice about visibility, not a shield from consequence. A released lot confirmed above its applicable limit on product represented as certified is never protected from a required change in its register status or from retailer, withdrawal, or recall action, whatever visibility the brand elected.



=== The readiness track, before certification


A brand whose product does not yet meet the standard is routed to the confidential readiness track rather than turned away, provided every tested lot meets the brand's applicable legal obligations. On the track the brand's data and progress are confidential, it receives the program's diagnostic and remediation support, and it may not use the mark on any product until a product meets the standard. The purpose is legal defensibility: participation, whether or not a mark is ever displayed, creates a timestamped, third-party-verified record of proactive improvement, which is the strongest available answer to the most exposed posture in litigation, the posture of having done nothing.



=== Corrective action on a certified product


When a certified product later fails a lot, the brand opens a corrective action, fixes the root cause, and re-tests. Separately from that correction, the brand chooses how the finding is disclosed. Under the confidential route, the default, the finding and its correction stay between the brand and the program while the item is open and on schedule, and register visibility, retailer disclosure, and the effect on the certificate follow the published status rules. Under the public route, elective, the brand publishes the finding and its remediation on its own brand page, producing a documented history of catching and fixing a problem rather than an unexplained gap. Neither route avoids the correction, and neither shields a released lot above its limit from a required register status change or from retailer, withdrawal, or recall action. A brand that conceals a known exceedance loses its certificate. The disclosure choice exists because a program that punished self-reporting would simply stop being told anything, which would make the mark worth less, not more.



== 2.6 Mark usage rules


Only SKUs at Status A or Status B may bear the mark. Status C, D, and E products, and products on the readiness track (Part 2.5), may not display it under any circumstances, and a brand must have at least one SKU at Status A or B before any use of the mark is permitted.


*Prohibited claims.* The mark certifies that a product was independently tested and met defined action levels under ongoing surveillance. It does not certify safety. A brand using the mark may not make claims that imply absolute safety, the absence of heavy metals, or zero risk. Claims such as "heavy metal free," "no toxic metals," or "100 percent safe from heavy metals" are specifically prohibited. The certified scope is a stated variable: the mark speaks to the product, form, and analyte panel actually tested, and not to a different form of the same product or to analytes outside the panel.


*Anti-arbitrage.* A brand that removes the mark from a SKU during a remediation period cannot resume use after a single passing lot. Re-entry to mark usage requires three consecutive passing lots at Status A or B levels and closure of any open corrective action. This prevents status arbitrage, in which a brand drops the mark during a bad period and re-enters when results improve by chance. A licensee that withdraws from the program entirely and re-enrolls within twenty-four months is handled under the exit-and-re-enter rule in Part 2.11.


*Visibility and publicity.* Certification and public listing are separable, and the choice between public-facing and private certification is the brand's, made under Part 2.5. Publicity is consent-based: the Program Operator does not list a certified product, publish its results, or publicize a brand's participation without the brand's authorization, given through the Certification Agreement, except as required by an enforcement action or a regulatory obligation. That authorization can be given or withheld per product, which is why the public register is not a census of every certified product.



== 2.7 Decision rules under measurement uncertainty


A laboratory result is a value with an uncertainty, and the program's pass and fail decisions incorporate the laboratory's reported expanded measurement uncertainty (MU) using a two-sided rule. A lot passes when the result plus MU is at or below the limit, so that even at the top of the uncertainty range the concentration does not exceed the action level. A lot fails when the result minus MU is above the limit, so that even at the bottom of the range it clearly exceeds. When neither condition holds, the result is borderline: the true value could sit on either side of the limit given the uncertainty.


No product is certified on a borderline result alone. Resolution requires at least one of a confirmatory re-analysis of the same extract, confirmatory testing of a new sample from the same lot under chain of custody, or lot hold-and-release pending resolution. The approach is documented, and the more conservative interpretation governs where ambiguity remains.


Two rules protect the integrity of this process. Repeated ad hoc retesting to obtain a passing result is prohibited: any retesting follows a pre-approved, scientifically justified, documented pathway, and undocumented resampling, lab-shopping, or selective reporting is a data-integrity violation. And when a laboratory does not report MU, the result is compared directly to the limit without adjustment; laboratories that cannot report MU are not preferred, and the Program Operator may require transition to one that does.



== 2.8 Data governance and integrity


The program's ability to demonstrate aggregate improvement over time, which is both the strongest legal defense for an individual brand and the strongest proof that the program achieves its public-health purpose, depends on structured, queryable data. Two requirements follow.


*Structured data.* Test results must be supplied in structured, machine-readable form, not as PDF documents, so that trend analysis, statistical evaluation, and population-level aggregation are possible. The brand authorizes the Program Operator in writing to access its laboratory data.


*Data integrity as a hard stop.* Falsification, manipulation, selective omission, or unauthorized alteration of test data triggers immediate suspension and likely revocation. Integrity violations include submitting falsified results, failing to report required results, using non-accredited laboratories without authorization, breaking chain of custody without documentation, and undisclosed retesting or lab-shopping. The Program Operator audits data integrity as part of routine oversight and may require random verification testing at designated laboratories.


*Laboratory requirements.* All testing used for certification decisions is performed by laboratories accredited to ISO/IEC 17025 for the relevant methods and food matrices, using methods fit for purpose. The laboratory's limit of quantitation for each analyte must be at or below the applicable program limit; where it is not, a non-detect result is not acceptable proof of compliance and a more sensitive method is required.



== 2.9 Corrective and preventive action


When a lot falls short, the brand opens a Corrective and Preventive Action (CAPA): a formal, documented process that identifies the root cause and implements both correction and prevention. Timelines scale with severity.


